
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">One day, I&#8217;m posting here about how to address AI risks, the next, Daily Nous suffers from a &#8220;critical error&#8221; and goes down for over a week. Coincidence? Or evidence that the AI&#8217;s thought I was onto something?</p>



<span id="more-22864"></span>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Yes I know it was the former&#8230; probably.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="612" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nuts-bolts-DN-1024x612.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-26090" role="none" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nuts-bolts-DN-1024x612.jpg 1024w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nuts-bolts-DN-300x179.jpg 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nuts-bolts-DN-768x459.jpg 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nuts-bolts-DN-1536x918.jpg 1536w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nuts-bolts-DN-2048x1224.jpg 2048w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nuts-bolts-DN-250x149.jpg 250w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">What happened? Daily Nous and its server were hacked. This isn&#8217;t the first time the site has had problems. A month earlier, the site was subject to what looks like some kind of attack. And at several times over recent years, various types of malware have infected the site, causing assorted difficulties. I employ security and clean-up services, but they face an increasing number and variety of threats. Meanwhile, the site has been aging&#8212;it&#8217;s over 14 years old&#8212;with some of its components becoming outdated, mutually incompatible, and difficult or impossible to update.  It had slowed down a bit, too.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">So when this last hack occurred, my technical consultant, John Hunt, recommended the site be rebuilt: taken apart, inspected piece by piece, problem elements fixed or replaced if necessary, and then put back together again, with even more security. &#8220;But what if we have to replace <em>all </em>of the pieces?&#8221; I asked. &#8220;Will it still be the same blog?&#8221; </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I wish I could say that the reason it has taken so long to get DN up and running again is that we were distracted by the metaphysics of blogular identity over time. But what really happened is that we just did what John recommended.  And by &#8220;we&#8221; I mean mostly John. At the start of the process we thought it would only take a few days, but as it continued, various further issues were discovered that needed addressing. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The result is not really a <em>redesign </em>of the site.  It should look roughly the same as it did before (despite that we had to ditch the original &#8220;theme&#8221;&#8211;the code that gave the site its look). But there are some functional improvements, and the site should run a little faster than it used to. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">We haven&#8217;t finished putting everything back on the site just yet. Some posts that had embedded videos or spreadsheets may be missing them, for example. <s>The Heap of Links is, fittingly enough, disordered at the moment.</s> The ad placements aren&#8217;t quite right. Some settings may still have to be properly adjusted. And we may yet notice some mistakes we need to correct.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">So things aren&#8217;t perfect yet, but, as you may have noticed over the years, I subscribe to the idea that one shouldn&#8217;t let the perfect be the enemy of the blog. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You&#8217;ve all been quite patient. Thanks for that.  And it seems you&#8217;ve all been quite cooperative, making barely any news while the site has been down, which I also appreciate. Or did I miss something? Let me know. </p>



